`mint version` reads intents and writes the version; `mint release` reads
only the manifest version and needs a `## <version>` changelog entry — it
never looks at `.release/`. The README showed the steady-state flow and
nothing about how a never-tagged repo gets to its first `v0.1.0`, so
adopters (repo-health, #17) conflated the two commands.

README gains two sections under Use:

- "First release": the invariant above, then two numbered bootstrap paths —
  by hand (set 0.1.0 + write the entry) or by intent (0.0.0 + `bump: minor`
  + `mint version`) — and the CI variant (merge, dispatch release.yml
  dry-run, then for real).
- "Tag creation under rulesets — the bypass actor": a ruleset restricting
  tag creation rejects `git push origin v0.1.0` for any actor not on its
  bypass list, admins included. Names the actor per path — the releaser's
  own account from a checkout, `github-actions[bot]` via GITHUB_TOKEN for
  the dispatched cut (not whoever clicked Run workflow) — and that a dry-run
  dispatch never exercises the push. Documents the rejection signature,
  that the tag + provenance already exist locally when the push fails (so
  `mint release` will refuse a re-run and the fix is `git push origin
  <tag>`), and `--no-push` as the deliberate hand-off to an authorised actor.

Plus a pointer from .release/README.md, a comment beside the push in
release-cut.yml naming the actor, and a patch intent.

Fixes #18.

